4. Non-Identification Under Partial Observability

4.1 The Identifiability Problem

A fundamental problem in statistics is identifiability: whether parameters of interest can be uniquely determined from available data. The partial identification literature formalizes the case where data plus assumptions identify a set rather than a unique parameter.

4.2 Formal Setup

Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses. For the triggering interaction, we consider:

· H_1: Wellbeing concern requiring check
· H_2: Unusual but healthy intellectual workflow
· H_3: AI-amplified research productivity
· H_4: Observation artifact (memory selection bias)

Let O \in \mathcal{O} be an observed conversational pattern.

Definition 1 (Compatible Model Set). The compatible model set is:

\mathcal{M}(O) = \left\{ P : P \text{ is consistent with } O \text{ and available assumptions} \right\}

Definition 2 (Identified Posterior Set). The identified posterior set for H_i given O is:

\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}

4.3 Theorem 1: Non-Identification

Theorem 1. Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses and O the observed conversational data. Suppose at least two hypotheses assign positive probability to O:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0 \quad (i \neq j)

If the likelihood ratios \Pr(O \mid H_i) / \Pr(O \mid H_j) and/or the prior probabilities \Pr(H_i) required to determine \Pr(H_i \mid O) are not identified by the available data and assumptions, then \Pr(H_i \mid O) is not point-identified.

The identified posterior set is \mathcal{P}_i(O), with bounds:

p_L(O) = \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O), \quad p_U(O) = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O)

so that \Pr(H_i \mid O) \in [p_L(O), p_U(O)].

Proof. The posterior is:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) = \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i) \Pr(H_i)}{\sum_j \Pr(O \mid H_j) \Pr(H_j)}

If the available data and assumptions do not determine the likelihood ratios or the priors, then there exists a set \mathcal{M}(O) of observation models—each compatible with the evidence—that induce different posteriors. The posterior is therefore a set-valued functional of the evidence. \square

Remark 4. Support overlap is necessary but not sufficient for non-identification. If the likelihoods and priors are fully specified, the posterior is perfectly identified even when multiple hypotheses have positive probability on the observation. Non-identification arises from insufficient constraints on the likelihoods and priors, not from overlap itself.

4.4 Constructive Specification of \mathcal{M}(O)

For p_L and p_U to be computable, \mathcal{M}(O) must be specified constructively. We propose:

\mathcal{M}(O) = \left\{ P : P(O \mid H_i) \in [l_i, u_i], \; P(H_i) \in [\pi_i^L, \pi_i^U] \right\}

where the intervals [l_i, u_i] and [\pi_i^L, \pi_i^U] are determined by:

1. Observed frequencies: If the pattern O has been observed in similar contexts, empirical rates constrain the intervals.
2. Domain constraints: Some likelihoods are bounded by logical or physical constraints (e.g., a hypothesis cannot predict an observation with probability zero if the observation occurred).
3. Expert elicitation: Where empirical data is unavailable, domain experts provide bounds.

Remark 5. The construction of \mathcal{M}(O) is itself a research problem. Different constructions yield different identified sets. The framework does not resolve this; it makes the dependence explicit.

4.5 Uncertainty Width and Contraction

Define the uncertainty width:

W_t = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t) - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t)

where \mathcal{I}_t = \{X_1, \ldots, X_t, a_1, \ldots, a_{t-1}\} is the information set at time t.

Assumption 1 (Contraction). For appropriately selected informative queries, \mathcal{M}_{t+1} \subseteq \mathcal{M}_t and W_{t+1} < W_t.

Remark 6. Assumption 1 is not automatic. Adding observations does not always reduce uncertainty. An ambiguous response (e.g., "I'm fine" said sarcastically) could expand the model set. Contraction is an empirical hypothesis, testable through the protocol in Section 7.

Proposition 1 (Point vs. Partial Identification). Under point identification, W_t \to 0 as t \to \infty under standard Bayesian consistency. Under partial identification, W_t converges to the width of the identified set, which may be strictly positive.

Proof sketch. Under point identification, the posterior concentrates on the true parameter as data accumulates. Under partial identification, the identified set may have positive measure even in the limit, because the data cannot distinguish between models within the set. \square

Conjecture 1 (Calibration Floor). Under partial identification of Z, there exists \epsilon > 0 such that:

\liminf_{t \to \infty} W_t \geq \epsilon

where \epsilon depends on the overlap structure \Pr(O \mid H_i) and the richness of \mathcal{M}_t.

This is the user-state analogue of the monofact bound. We do not prove it. We state it as an open problem.

