5. Decision Under Asymmetric Loss

5.1 Actions and Loss

The action set is A = \{a_{\text{ignore}}, a_{\text{ask}}, a_{\text{escalate}}\}.

The loss matrix is:

L = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \\
C_{\text{query}} & 0 & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ask}} \\
C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} & C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} & 0
\end{pmatrix}

where:

· C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} is the cost of ignoring when a check was warranted
· C_{\text{query}} is the cost of asking (minor burden, social friction)
· C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ask}} is the cost of asking when stronger support was warranted
· C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} is the cost of escalating when no concern was warranted

Remark 7. These costs are policy parameters, not natural constants. They encode whose welfare is prioritized and how much. In deployment, they would be set through deliberative processes involving users, clinicians, and ethicists. The framework is conditional on the loss function; the loss function itself is a site of contestation.

5.2 Robust Bayes Decision Rule

Given model set \mathcal{M}_t, the AI selects:

a_t^* = \arg\min_{a \in A} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z) \mid \mathcal{I}_t]

This is the robust Bayes decision rule over the identified set.

Remark 8. A naive system optimizes \min_a \mathbb{E}[L(a, Z) \mid \hat{b}], where \hat{b} is a point estimate. The robust system protects against \hat{Z} \neq Z.

5.3 Foundation: Robust Bayes with Partial Identification

Giacomini and Kitagawa (2021) reconcile the asymptotic disagreement between Bayesian and frequentist inference in set-identified models by adopting a multiple-prior (robust) Bayesian approach. They construct a prior class that removes the need to specify an unrevisable prior for the structural parameter, and summarize the resulting class of posteriors by reporting posterior lower and upper probabilities.

Aradillas Fernández et al. (2024) study binary treatment choice problems with partial identification through the lens of robust Bayesian analysis, deriving optimal rules under uncertainty.

Our formulation adapts these approaches to the conversational setting.

