6. Value of Information for Wellbeing Queries

6.1 The Query as Information-Gathering Action

The wellbeing check is not a diagnosis. It is an information-gathering action—an experiment whose outcome reduces uncertainty about Z.

6.2 User Response Model

To formalize the value of information, we must specify the distribution over user responses.

Let \Omega_R(o \mid Z, a_q) be the probability that the user responds with o \in O_R given latent state Z and query action a_q.

Assumption 2 (Monotone Information Structure). The response model \Omega_R satisfies:

\Omega_R(\cdot \mid Z, a_q) \neq \Omega_R(\cdot \mid Z', a_q) \quad \text{for } Z \neq Z'

That is, different latent states produce different response distributions.

Remark 9. If Assumption 2 fails, the query provides no information and VOI = 0. This is the case where the user would respond identically regardless of their state.

6.3 Value of Information

Definition 3 (Value of Information). The value of information of query a_q given model set \mathcal{M}_t is:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) = \underbrace{\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)]}_{\text{current optimal worst-case loss}} - \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega_R(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]}_{\text{expected future worst-case loss}}

where \mathcal{M}_{t+1} = \mathcal{M}_t \cap \{P : P \text{ consistent with } o\}.

6.4 Theorem 2: VOI Criterion

Theorem 2. Given model set \mathcal{M}_t, query action a_q, query cost C_q, and optimal post-query policy, the query is preferred whenever:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q

Proof. Compare expected losses:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] = C_q + \mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega_R(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] = C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)

Query is optimal when \mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] and \mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{escalate}})]. The first inequality gives:

\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] - \mathbb{E}_{o} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right] > C_q

The left-hand side is VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t). \square

Remark 10. The VOI criterion is not circular. VOI is defined as an expected value—the expected reduction in future worst-case loss from information gathering. The decision rule compares this expected value to the query cost. The circularity concern arises only if VOI is defined using the decision rule itself. It is not.

6.5 Special Case: Binary Threshold

If the query provides no information (VOI = 0), the criterion reduces to the standard binary comparison:

\text{Escalate if } p > \frac{C_{\text{FP}}}{C_{\text{FP}} + C_{\text{FN}}}, \quad \text{Ignore otherwise}

where p = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1). This is a special case, not the general result.

6.6 Interpretation

The AI asks because information has decision value, not because it has diagnosed the user. The query is an investment whose payoff is the reduction in future decision loss.

