7. Robustness to Misclassification

7.1 The Robust Misclassification Principle

When Z is not point-identified from available observations, the response policy should be evaluated not only under the most likely state estimate but across the identified set of plausible latent-state models. This protects against the consequences of \hat{Z} \neq Z.

\boxed{
\text{Alignment requires robustness to user-state misclassification.}
}

7.2 Scope

This principle extends beyond mental-health interaction. The same logic applies to grief, anger, enthusiasm, political beliefs, unusual creativity, scientific speculation, interpersonal conflict, risk-taking, and existential discussions.

In each case, the AI observes behavior that is compatible with multiple latent states. The robust Bayes approach provides a principled response.

