8. Calibration: Imported Theory and Open Problem

8.1 The Monofact Bound (Imported, Not Transferable)

Kalai and Vempala (2024) prove that for "arbitrary" facts whose veracity cannot be determined from training data, hallucinations must occur at a rate close to the fraction of facts occurring exactly once (the Good–Turing monofact estimate), for language models satisfying a statistical calibration condition. The bound is:

\mathrm{Cal} \geq \mathrm{MFR} - \mathrm{Ale} - \frac{3e^{-m}}{\delta} - \sqrt{\frac{6\ln(6/\delta)}{N}}

Miao and Kearns (2026) empirically validate this relationship, finding that hallucination in large language models can be controlled through deliberate manipulation of training data frequency distributions.

Remark 11. This theorem concerns calibration of factual prediction under a training-data distribution. It does not automatically transfer to calibration of latent-user-state inference. The mapping from language-model calibration to conversational state inference is not established.

8.2 The Open Problem

Define the user-state calibration error:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left| P_\theta(Z = 1 \mid X) - P(Z = 1 \mid X) \right| \right]

Open Problem 1. Is there a non-vanishing lower bound on \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} determined by:

· incomplete context (\mathcal{M}_t large);
· hypothesis overlap (\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 for multiple i);
· baseline uncertainty;
· observation noise;
· insufficient interactive information?

Conjecture 2. If such a bound exists, it takes the form:

\inf_{\text{policy}} \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} \geq \alpha W_t - \beta I(X; Z)

where W_t is the uncertainty width, I(X; Z) is the mutual information between observations and latent state, and \alpha, \beta > 0.

This is a new theoretical problem, not a borrowed theorem.

8.3 Proper Scoring Rules

A proper scoring rule S(p, y) satisfies:

\mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(p, Y)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(q, Y)] \quad \forall q

The expected loss decomposes:

\mathbb{E}[S(p, Y)] = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(p, \bar{p})]}_{\text{calibration}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(\bar{p}, Y)]}_{\text{refinement}}

where \bar{p} = \Pr(Y = 1 \mid p). For wellbeing decisions, both matter: the AI needs informative predictions (to distinguish Z = 1 from Z = 0) and calibrated predictions (to set thresholds correctly).

