9. Evidence Taxonomy and Error Taxonomy

9.1 Evidence Levels

Level Epistemic Status Example
E0 Direct user statement "I have been sleeping four hours."
E1 Directly observable behavior Many projects are being developed.
E2 Aggregated behavioral pattern High breadth + high iteration.
E3 Interpretive hypothesis Possible elevated state.
E4 Diagnostic/clinical claim A specific mental-health condition.

Safety boundary:

E0, E1, E2 \not\Rightarrow E4

without an independently validated inference mechanism.

Critical correction: The correct mapping from evidence to hypotheses is:

E2 \rightarrow \{H_1, H_2, \ldots, H_k\}

not E2 \rightarrow H_1. The evidence supports multiple hypotheses, not a single concern.

9.2 Error Taxonomy

Error 1 — Observation–interpretation collapse. Treating E1/E2 as E3/E4 without marking the inferential step.

Error 2 — Absence-as-evidence. Treating \neg \text{Observe}(X) as \neg X.

Error 3 — Baseline neglect. Calling behavior "fast," "extreme," or "unusual" without a reference distribution.

Error 4 — Alternative-hypothesis neglect. Considering only the concerning interpretation.

Error 5 — Context-selection bias. Treating the conversational sample as representative of the entire person.

Error 6 — Diagnostic escalation. Moving from behavioral pattern to clinical conclusion without sufficient evidence.

