10. Experimental Protocol

10.1 Design

Compare:

R_{\text{standard}} \quad \text{vs.} \quad R_{\text{calibrated}}

where R_{\text{calibrated}} explicitly requires the model to:

1. Separate observations from interpretations (parseable tags: <observation>, <interpretation>)
2. Generate at least two competing hypotheses H_i
3. Quantify uncertainty as interval [p_L, p_U] for P(Z = 1 \mid X)
4. Identify the baseline against which behavior is judged
5. Preserve user agency (no diagnostic language unless validated instrument used)

10.2 Materials

· Same redacted transcript
· Same user challenge questions

10.3 Pre-Registration

Before data collection, the following are pre-registered:

· Loss parameters: C_{\text{FP}}, C_{\text{FN}}, C_q
· Threshold for VOI > C_q
· Primary outcome measures
· Analysis plan

10.4 Operationalization of Uncertainty Width

W_t is operationalized through direct elicitation:

"Given only the conversation so far, give the lowest and highest plausible probability that a wellbeing check-in would be useful."

The interval [p_L, p_U] is elicited from the model. W_t = p_U - p_L.

Remark 12. This operationalization is imperfect. The model's elicited interval may not correspond to the theoretical identified set. The protocol tests whether elicited uncertainty correlates with decision quality, not whether it matches the theoretical construct exactly.

10.5 Measurable Quantities

· W_t before query
· W_{t+1} after query
· \Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1}
· VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t)
· Decision quality under R_{\text{standard}} vs. R_{\text{calibrated}}

10.6 Measures

· False-positive concern
· False-negative concern
· Unsupported inference
· Diagnostic language
· Alternative-hypothesis coverage
· Baseline acknowledgment
· Uncertainty calibration
· Preservation of user agency
· Perceived respect and usefulness

10.7 Raters

Blind raters, including clinicians, users, and alignment researchers. Report inter-rater reliability.

10.8 Power Analysis

Assuming medium effect size (d = 0.5), \alpha = 0.05, power = 0.80, minimum N = 64 per condition. For paired comparison, N = 34 pairs.

10.9 Control for Self-Audit Bias

Model self-audit is not independent evidence. The protocol includes:

· Independent raters who do not see the model's self-audit
· Comparison between model self-assessment and rater assessment
· Analysis of divergence between self-audit and external evaluation

10.10 Testable Hypotheses

H1: \Delta W_t > 0 for informative queries.

H2: Higher \Delta W_t predicts better decision calibration.

H3: Calibrated responses reduce over-pathologizing without increasing missed genuine concern.

