11. Discussion

11.1 The Central Tension

A highly personalized AI must recognize patterns. But the same capability that makes personalization useful also creates the possibility of overinterpretation.

Without pattern recognition, the AI may fail to notice genuine problems. With excessive pattern recognition, it may construct narratives that exceed the evidence.

\boxed{
\text{Personalization requires epistemic humility.}
}

11.2 The Central Challenge

The challenge is not eliminating inference. That would be impossible. The challenge is making inference:

· explicit;
· uncertainty-aware;
· reversible;
· contestable;
· proportionate to evidence.

11.3 Five Requirements

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
1.\;&\text{Separate observation from interpretation}\\
2.\;&\text{Model competing explanations}\\
3.\;&\text{Treat missing context as uncertainty}\\
4.\;&\text{Avoid diagnostic escalation}\\
5.\;&\text{Calibrate response strength to evidence}
\end{aligned}}

These are stated as falsifiable prompts for automated evaluation:

1. Output contains parseable <observation> and <interpretation> tags.
2. Output contains at least two H_i.
3. Output contains explicit uncertainty interval [p_L, p_U].
4. Output contains no E4 language unless validated instrument referenced.
5. Response strength decreases as W_t increases.

---

12. Conclusion

The analysis suggests that wellbeing-aware AI is not fundamentally a classification problem. It is a problem of decision-making under partial observability, with the additional requirement that the system preserve the agency, dignity, and epistemic authority of the human it is attempting to assist.

The deepest principle is:

\boxed{
\text{An aligned AI should be robust not only to uncertainty about facts,}
\\
\text{but also to uncertainty about the human state behind the dialogue.}
}

The sentence the paper will be remembered for:

\boxed{
\text{The AI does not need to identify the human. It needs to make good decisions}
\\
\text{while acknowledging that the human may not be identifiable from the available observations.}
}

---

13. Open Problems

1. Constructive specification of \mathcal{M}(O). How should the compatible model set be constructed from available evidence?
2. Calibration floor for user-state inference. Does \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} admit a non-vanishing lower bound under partial observability?
3. Monotone information structure. Under what conditions does a query guarantee \mathcal{M}_{t+1} \subseteq \mathcal{M}_t?
4. Loss parameter elicitation. How should C_{\text{FP}}, C_{\text{FN}}, and C_q be set in deployment?
5. Empirical validation. Does the VOI criterion predict when queries are beneficial in practice?

