Appendix B — Full Proof of Theorem 1

B.1 Setup

Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses with positive prior probability. Let O \in \mathcal{O} be an observed conversational pattern. Let \mathcal{M}(O) be the set of observation models compatible with O and the available assumptions.

Definition B.1 (Compatible Model Set). The compatible model set is:

\mathcal{M}(O) = \left\{ P : P \text{ is consistent with } O \text{ and available assumptions} \right\}

A constructive specification is:

\mathcal{M}(O) = \left\{ P : P(O \mid H_i) \in [l_i, u_i] \;\forall i, \; P(H_i) \in [\pi_i^L, \pi_i^U], \; \sum_i P(H_i) = 1 \right\}

where the intervals [l_i, u_i] and [\pi_i^L, \pi_i^U] are determined by observed frequencies, domain constraints, or expert elicitation.

B.2 Theorem 1

Theorem 1. Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses and O the observed conversational data. Suppose at least two hypotheses assign positive probability to O:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0 \quad (i \neq j)

If the likelihood ratios \Pr(O \mid H_i) / \Pr(O \mid H_j) and/or the prior probabilities \Pr(H_i) required to determine \Pr(H_i \mid O) are not identified by the available data and assumptions, then \Pr(H_i \mid O) is not point-identified.

Proof. The posterior probability is given by Bayes' rule:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) = \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i) \Pr(H_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^k \Pr(O \mid H_j) \Pr(H_j)}

This posterior is a function f: \mathbb{R}_{+}^k \times \Delta^{k-1} \to [0,1] of the likelihood vector (\Pr(O \mid H_j))_{j=1}^k and the prior vector (\Pr(H_j))_{j=1}^k.

If the available data and assumptions do not determine the likelihood ratios or the priors, then there exist two models P, P' \in \mathcal{M}(O) such that either:

\frac{P(O \mid H_i)}{P(O \mid H_j)} \neq \frac{P'(O \mid H_i)}{P'(O \mid H_j)}

or:

P(H_i) \neq P'(H_i)

In either case, P(H_i \mid O) \neq P'(H_i \mid O), because the posterior is strictly monotone in the likelihood ratio and in the prior.

Therefore, the posterior is a set-valued functional of the evidence:

\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}

The identified set is non-singleton (p_L < p_U) whenever there exist two compatible models that induce different posteriors. The bounds are:

p_L(O) = \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O), \quad p_U(O) = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O)

so that \Pr(H_i \mid O) \in [p_L(O), p_U(O)]. \square

B.3 Corollaries

Corollary B.1. The statement "the pattern is worth checking" is epistemically valid. The statement "the pattern indicates a wellbeing concern" is not, because it requires collapsing non-identifiable hypotheses into a single diagnostic conclusion.

Corollary B.2. When contextual information is incomplete, the set \mathcal{M}(O) is larger, and the identified set [p_L, p_U] is potentially wider. This relationship is an empirical hypothesis, not an assumption.

Corollary B.3. For the observation pattern O = \{\text{high breadth} + \text{high pace} + \text{limited observed external validation}\}, each of the four hypotheses H_1–H_4 (wellbeing concern, healthy workflow, AI-amplified productivity, observation artifact) assigns positive probability. Therefore, O is non-identifying.

B.4 Remark on the Necessity of Insufficient Constraints

Support overlap is necessary but not sufficient for non-identification. If the likelihoods \Pr(O \mid H_i) and priors \Pr(H_i) are fully specified, the posterior is perfectly identified even when multiple hypotheses have positive probability on O. Non-identification arises from insufficient constraints on the likelihoods and priors, not from overlap itself.

