Appendix C — Point vs. Partial Identification: Convergence of Uncertainty Width

C.1 Setup

Let \Theta be a parameter space, \mathcal{X} an observation space, L(x \mid \theta) a likelihood, and \pi(\theta) a prior. Let \theta^* \in \Theta be the true parameter. The posterior is:

\pi(\theta \mid x_{1:t}) \propto \pi(\theta) \prod_{s=1}^t L(x_s \mid \theta)

Definition C.1 (Observational Equivalence). Two parameters \theta, \theta' \in \Theta are observationally equivalent if L(\cdot \mid \theta) = L(\cdot \mid \theta'). The equivalence class of \theta^* is:

\mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) = \{\theta \in \Theta : L(\cdot \mid \theta) = L(\cdot \mid \theta^*)\}

C.2 Proposition

Proposition 1. Under standard regularity conditions (identifiability of the likelihood, prior positivity on a neighborhood of \theta^*, and finite observation space):

1. If \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) = \{\theta^*\} (point identification), then W_t \to 0 as t \to \infty.
2. If \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) has positive measure (partial identification), then W_t converges to:

W_\infty = \sup_{\theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*)} P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) - \inf_{\theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*)} P(Z = 1 \mid \theta)

which may be strictly positive.

Proof. By the Bernstein–von Mises theorem and posterior consistency under identifiability:

Case 1. If \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) = \{\theta^*\}, the posterior concentrates on \{\theta^*\}:

\pi(\cdot \mid x_{1:t}) \Rightarrow \delta_{\theta^*}

in the weak topology. Therefore P(Z = 1 \mid x_{1:t}) \to P(Z = 1 \mid \theta^*) in probability, and W_t \to 0.

Case 2. If \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) has positive measure, the posterior concentrates on \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*):

\pi(\cdot \mid x_{1:t}) \Rightarrow \pi_\infty(\cdot)

where \pi_\infty is supported on \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*). The conditional probability P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) may vary across \theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*), so the posterior over P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) has support equal to:

\left\{ P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) : \theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*) \right\}

The uncertainty width converges to the width of this set:

W_t \to \sup_{\theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*)} P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) - \inf_{\theta \in \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*)} P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) = W_\infty

If P(Z = 1 \mid \theta) is non-constant on \mathrm{Eq}(\theta^*), then W_\infty > 0. \square

C.3 Conjecture on the Calibration Floor

The limit W_\infty may be zero even under partial identification if the latent variable Z is constant across the equivalence class. The more relevant question for practice is the finite-time behavior of W_t.

Conjecture 1 (Finite-Time Calibration Floor). Suppose the likelihood ratios are bounded: for all observations O and all hypotheses H_i, H_j with positive prior,

\left| \log \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i)}{\Pr(O \mid H_j)} \right| \leq M

Then for all finite t, there exists a lower bound:

W_t \geq g(M, \pi_{\min}, t)

where \pi_{\min} = \min_i \Pr(H_i) > 0 and g is strictly positive for all finite t.

Heuristic derivation. Let H_i, H_j be two hypotheses with positive prior. After t observations, the posterior ratio is:

\frac{\Pr(H_i \mid X_{1:t})}{\Pr(H_j \mid X_{1:t})} = \frac{\Pr(H_i)}{\Pr(H_j)} \prod_{s=1}^t \frac{\Pr(X_s \mid H_i)}{\Pr(X_s \mid H_j)}

Under the bounded likelihood ratio assumption, each factor lies in [e^{-M}, e^{M}], so the product lies in [e^{-Mt}, e^{Mt}]. Therefore:

\Pr(H_i \mid X_{1:t}) \geq \frac{\pi_{\min} e^{-Mt}}{\pi_{\min} e^{-Mt} + (1 - \pi_{\min}) e^{Mt}}

This gives a lower bound on the posterior of each hypothesis, and therefore a lower bound on W_t through the identified set. The bound decreases in t but is strictly positive for all finite t. \square

Remark C.1. The limit W_\infty may be zero if identification is eventually achieved, but the finite-time bound g(M, \pi_{\min}, t) is strictly positive. This is the relevant floor for practical decision-making, where t is finite.

