Appendix D — Full Proof of Theorem 2

D.1 Setup

Let \mathcal{M}_t be the compatible model set at time t. Let a_q be a query action with cost C_q. Let \Omega_R(o \mid Z, a_q) be the user response model, satisfying Assumption 2 (monotone information structure).

Let \mathcal{M}_{t+1}(o) = \mathcal{M}_t \cap \{P : P \text{ consistent with } o\} be the updated model set after observing response o.

D.2 Theorem 2

Theorem 2. Given model set \mathcal{M}_t, query action a_q, query cost C_q, and optimal post-query policy, the query is preferred over the optimal non-query action whenever:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q

Proof. Define the current optimal worst-case loss over non-query actions:

V_0 = \min_{a \in \{a_{\text{ignore}}, a_{\text{escalate}}\}} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)]

Define the expected future optimal worst-case loss after querying:

V_1 = \mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega_R(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_{a \in A} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}(o)} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]

The expected loss of querying is:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] = C_q + V_1

The expected loss of the best non-query action is V_0. Querying is preferred iff:

C_q + V_1 < V_0

Rearranging:

V_0 - V_1 > C_q

The left-hand side is by definition VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t). Therefore querying is preferred iff VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q.

It remains to verify that querying is also preferred over all other actions. Since V_0 is the minimum over non-query actions, and querying is preferred over the minimizing non-query action, it is preferred over all non-query actions. \square

D.3 Special Case: Binary Threshold

If the query provides no information, then \mathcal{M}_{t+1}(o) = \mathcal{M}_t for all o, so V_1 = V_0 and VOI = 0. The criterion VOI > C_q fails, and the decision reduces to the comparison between a_{\text{ignore}} and a_{\text{escalate}}:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] = C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{escalate}})] = C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} \cdot \left(1 - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)\right)

Escalate is preferred iff:

C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} \cdot (1 - p) < C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot p

where p = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1) is the worst-case probability of needing support. Rearranging:

p > \frac{C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}}}{C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} + C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}}}

This is the standard binary threshold. It is a special case, not the general result.

D.4 Remark on Circularity

The definition of VOI is not circular. VOI is defined as an expected value—the expected reduction in future worst-case loss from information gathering. The decision rule compares this expected value to the query cost. Circularity would arise only if VOI were defined using the decision rule itself. It is not.

D.5 Remark on Monotone Information Structure

Assumption 2 (monotone information structure) ensures that the query provides information: different latent states produce different response distributions. If Assumption 2 fails, \Omega_R(\cdot \mid Z, a_q) is independent of Z, and the query provides no information. In that case VOI = 0, and the query is never strictly preferred.

