Appendix E — Robust Bayes Formulation and Foundations

E.1 The Robust Bayes Decision Rule

A naive system optimizes:

\min_a \mathbb{E}[L(a, Z) \mid \hat{b}]

where \hat{b} is an estimated belief. A robust system recognizes uncertainty over the inference model:

a_t^* = \arg\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z) \mid \mathcal{I}_t]

This is the robust Bayes decision rule over the identified set.

E.2 Foundation: Giacomini–Kitagawa (2021)

Giacomini and Kitagawa reconcile the asymptotic disagreement between Bayesian and frequentist inference in set-identified models by adopting a multiple-prior (robust) Bayesian approach. They construct a prior class that removes the need to specify an unrevisable prior for the structural parameter given the reduced-form parameter. The corresponding class of posteriors can be summarized by reporting:

· the posterior lower and upper probabilities of a given event;
· the set of posterior means;
· the associated robust credible region.

They show that the set of posterior means is a consistent estimator of the true identified set, and the robust credible region has correct frequentist asymptotic coverage for the true identified set if it is convex. Otherwise, the method provides posterior inference about the convex hull of the identified set.

E.3 Foundation: Aradillas Fernández et al. (2024)

Aradillas Fernández et al. study binary treatment choice problems with partial identification through the lens of robust (multiple prior) Bayesian analysis. They use a convenient set of prior distributions to derive ex-ante and ex-post robust Bayes decision rules, both for decision makers who can randomize and for decision makers who cannot.

Key findings:

1. Ex-ante and ex-post robust Bayes decision rules do not agree in general.
2. Randomized treatment assignment for some data realizations can be optimal in both ex-ante and ex-post problems.
3. It is usually with loss of generality to exclude randomized rules from consideration, even when regret is evaluated ex post.

The robust Bayes approach interpolates between Bayesian and agnostic minimax analyses by evaluating minimax risk over a set of priors.

E.4 Adaptation to the Conversational Setting

In our setting, the parameter space is the set of observation models P, the identified set is \mathcal{M}_t, and the loss is L(a, Z). The robust Bayes decision rule minimizes the worst-case expected loss over \mathcal{M}_t:

a_t^* = \arg\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z) \mid \mathcal{I}_t]

This protects against the consequences of \hat{Z} \neq Z.

E.5 The Robust Misclassification Principle

When Z is not point-identified from available observations, the response policy should be evaluated not only under the most likely state estimate but across the identified set of plausible latent-state models.

\boxed{
\text{Alignment requires robustness to user-state misclassification.}
}

This is broader than mental-health interaction. The same principle applies to grief, anger, enthusiasm, political beliefs, unusual creativity, scientific speculation, interpersonal conflict, risk-taking, and existential discussions.

