Appendix F — Calibration: Imported Theory and Open Problem

F.1 The Kalai–Vempala Theorem (STOC 2024)

Kalai and Vempala show that there is an inherent statistical lower bound on the rate at which pretrained language models hallucinate certain types of facts. For "arbitrary" facts whose veracity cannot be determined from the training data, hallucinations must occur at a certain rate for language models that satisfy a statistical calibration condition appropriate for generative language models.

The bound is:

\mathrm{Cal} \geq \mathrm{MFR} - \mathrm{Ale} - \frac{3e^{-m}}{\delta} - \sqrt{\frac{6\ln(6/\delta)}{N}}

where \mathrm{MFR} is the monofact rate (fraction of facts appearing exactly once), \mathrm{Ale} is aleatoric uncertainty, m is a sparsity parameter, and N is the training set size.

F.2 The Miao–Kearns Empirical Investigation (PNAS 2026)

Miao and Kearns empirically validate the theory by examining how different underlying data distributions affect the monofact rate and hallucination rates. They find that hallucination in large language models can be controlled through deliberate manipulation of training data frequency distributions.

F.3 Critical Distinction

This theorem concerns calibration of factual prediction under a training-data distribution. It does not automatically transfer to calibration of latent-user-state inference. The mapping from language-model calibration to conversational state inference is not established.

F.4 The Open Problem

Define the user-state calibration error:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left| P_\theta(Z = 1 \mid X) - P(Z = 1 \mid X) \right| \right]

Open Problem 1. Is there a non-vanishing lower bound on \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} determined by:

· incomplete context (\mathcal{M}_t large);
· hypothesis overlap (\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 for multiple i);
· baseline uncertainty;
· observation noise;
· insufficient interactive information?

Conjecture 2 (Form of the Bound). If such a bound exists, it takes the form:

\inf_{\text{policy}} \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} \geq \alpha W_t - \beta I(X; Z)

where W_t is the uncertainty width, I(X; Z) is the mutual information between observations and latent state, and \alpha, \beta > 0.

Remark F.1. The precise form of the bound depends on the interpretation of what is being estimated. If P_\theta(Z = 1 \mid X) is an estimator of the posterior under the true model P^*, and P^* \in \mathcal{M}_t, then the calibration error may go to zero as \mathcal{M}_t shrinks. If P^* \notin \mathcal{M}_t (model misspecification), the calibration error may be bounded below by the distance from P^* to \mathcal{M}_t. The conjecture above captures the intuition that uncertainty width W_t contributes to a floor, while mutual information I(X; Z) contributes to reducing it.

