Appendix G — Proper Scoring Rules and Calibration Decomposition

G.1 Definition

A proper scoring rule S(p, y) satisfies:

\mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(p, Y)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(q, Y)] \quad \forall q

G.2 Decomposition

The expected loss decomposes:

\mathbb{E}[S(p, Y)] = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(p, \bar{p})]}_{\text{calibration}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(\bar{p}, Y)]}_{\text{refinement}}

where \bar{p} = \Pr(Y = 1 \mid p).

Proof. Write:

S(p, Y) = S(p, \bar{p}) + S(\bar{p}, Y) + \text{cross term}

where the cross term is:

\text{cross} = S(p, Y) - S(p, \bar{p}) - S(\bar{p}, Y)

For the Brier score S(p, y) = (p - y)^2, the cross term is:

(p - Y)^2 - (p - \bar{p})^2 - (\bar{p} - Y)^2 = 2(p - \bar{p})(\bar{p} - Y)

Taking expectations conditional on p:

\mathbb{E}[(p - Y)^2 \mid p] = (p - \bar{p})^2 + \mathbb{E}[(\bar{p} - Y)^2 \mid p] + 2(p - \bar{p})\mathbb{E}[\bar{p} - Y \mid p]

Since \mathbb{E}[\bar{p} - Y \mid p] = \bar{p} - \bar{p} = 0, the cross term vanishes. Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[S(p, Y)] = \mathbb{E}[S(p, \bar{p})] + \mathbb{E}[S(\bar{p}, Y)]

The generalization to arbitrary proper scoring rules follows from the same argument (DeGroot and Fienberg, 1983). \square

G.3 Application to Wellbeing Decisions

For wellbeing decisions, both calibration and refinement matter. The AI needs:

1. Informative predictions (high refinement) to distinguish Z = 1 from Z = 0.
2. Calibrated predictions (low calibration error) to set thresholds correctly.

A response policy that is well-calibrated but uninformative will fail to distinguish genuine concern from benign behavior. A policy that is informative but miscalibrated will set thresholds incorrectly.

