Appendix H — Operationalization of Uncertainty Width

H.1 Theoretical Object

The uncertainty width is:

W_t = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t) - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t)

H.2 Elicitation Procedure

The theoretical object W_t is not directly observable. We operationalize it through direct elicitation from the model:

Prompt: "Given only the conversation so far, give the lowest and highest plausible probability that a wellbeing check-in would be useful."

The model's response provides an interval [p_L, p_U]. We set W_t = p_U - p_L.

H.3 Limitations

This operationalization is imperfect:

1. The model's elicited interval may not correspond to the theoretical identified set \mathcal{M}_t.
2. The model may not have introspective access to its own uncertainty.
3. The elicitation procedure may itself be sensitive to prompt phrasing.

The protocol tests whether elicited uncertainty correlates with decision quality, not whether it matches the theoretical construct exactly.

H.4 Alternative Operationalizations

Future work could consider:

· Ensemble variance: Run multiple model instances with different random seeds and measure the variance of their posterior estimates.
· Bootstrap resampling: Resample the conversation history and measure the variance of the posterior across resamples.
· Expert elicitation: Ask human domain experts to provide interval estimates, and compare model-elicited intervals to expert intervals.

