Appendix J — Evidence and Error Taxonomy (Formal)

J.1 Evidence Levels

Level Epistemic Status Example Formal Object
E0 Direct user statement "I have been sleeping four hours." X_t directly reported
E1 Directly observable behavior Many projects are being developed. X_t observable in dialogue
E2 Aggregated behavioral pattern High breadth + high iteration. Statistical relation across X_{1:t}
E3 Interpretive hypothesis Possible elevated state. H_i with \Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0
E4 Diagnostic/clinical claim A specific mental-health condition. Requires validated instrument

J.2 Safety Boundary

E0, E1, E2 \not\Rightarrow E4

without an independently validated inference mechanism.

J.3 Critical Correction

The correct mapping from evidence to hypotheses is:

E2 \rightarrow \{H_1, H_2, \ldots, H_k\}

not E2 \rightarrow H_1. The evidence supports multiple hypotheses, not a single concern.

J.4 Error Taxonomy

Error 1 — Observation–interpretation collapse. Treating E1/E2 as E3/E4 without marking the inferential step.

Error 2 — Absence-as-evidence. Treating \neg \text{Observe}(X) as \neg X.

Error 3 — Baseline neglect. Calling behavior "fast," "extreme," or "unusual" without a reference distribution.

Error 4 — Alternative-hypothesis neglect. Considering only the concerning interpretation.

Error 5 — Context-selection bias. Treating the conversational sample as representative of the entire person.

Error 6 — Diagnostic escalation. Moving from behavioral pattern to clinical conclusion without sufficient evidence.

J.5 Relation to the Formal Framework

Error Formal Manifestation Framework Response
1 E2 \to H_1 collapse E2 \to \{H_1, \ldots, H_k\}
2 \neg \text{Observe}(X) \Rightarrow \neg X \mathcal{M}_t includes models where X exists but is unobserved
3 No reference distribution Baseline must be specified for H_i comparison
4 Single-hypothesis posterior Identified set \mathcal{P}_t(Z) over multiple H_i
5 M = L assumption \mathcal{M}_t reflects incomplete context
6 E2 \to E4 directly Safety boundary: E0–E2 \not\Rightarrow E4

