   CRITICAL DISTINCTION:

     Support overlap IS NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT for non-identification.

     If likelihoods Pr(O|H_i) and priors Pr(H_i) are FULLY SPECIFIED,
     the posterior is PERFECTLY IDENTIFIED even with overlap.

     Non-identification requires INSUFFICIENT CONSTRAINTS on the
     likelihoods or priors — not mere overlap.

   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   FORMAL STATEMENT (Theorem 1):

     If  Pr(O|H_i) > 0 and Pr(O|H_j) > 0  for i ≠ j
     AND the likelihood ratios Pr(O|H_i)/Pr(O|H_j)
         and/or priors Pr(H_i) are NOT IDENTIFIED,
     THEN  Pr(H_i | O) is NOT POINT-IDENTIFIED.

     The posterior lies in  P_i(O) = { Pr(H_i|O) : P ∈ M(O) }
     with bounds  Pr(H_i|O) ∈ [p_L(O), p_U(O)].
